course: biology
lesson: 75
title: Dinosaurs: kinds, sizes and how they lived
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dinosaurs ruled the land for about 160 million years, from about 230 to 66 million years ago, and they came in every size, from the chicken-sized Compsognathus to Diplodocus, longer than two buses.
explain: Stand under the open jaws of a T. rex skull in the museum and you see a bone-crusher: teeth like bananas and one of the strongest bites of any land animal that ever lived, but arms only about a metre long.
explain: Plant-eaters were not helpless: Stegosaurus had tail spikes, Triceratops horns and a frill, and Ankylosaurus armour down to its eyelids and a club that could break a leg bone.
explain: Films and cartoons get a lot wrong: real Velociraptor was turkey-sized and feathered, pterosaurs and plesiosaurs were not dinosaurs at all, and no person ever saw a living T. rex.
fact: Tyrannosaurus rex lived about 68 to 66 million years ago in western North America, was about 12 metres long and had thick teeth up to about 30 centimetres with the root, able to crush bone.
fact: T. rex arms were about 1 metre long with two fingers each, but thick and muscular, probably able to lift more than a grown man; scientists still argue about what they were used for.
fact: Stegosaurus was a plant-eater about 9 metres long that lived about 150 million years ago, with two rows of bony back plates and four tail spikes; the idea of a second brain in its hips is a myth.
fact: Triceratops was a plant-eater about 9 metres long with three horns and a bony frill, living at the same time and place as T. rex; some fossils have healed T. rex bite marks.
fact: Long-necked sauropods were the biggest land animals ever: Diplodocus was about 26 metres long, and Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan may have weighed as much as ten elephants or more; a long neck let them reach lots of plants while their heavy bodies stood still, which saved energy.
fact: Velociraptor lived in Mongolia about 75 million years ago, was about the size of a turkey and had feathers; a 1971 fossil shows one locked in a fight with a Protoceratops.
fact: Pterosaurs such as Quetzalcoatlus, with a wingspan of about 10 metres, had wings of skin on one long finger and were not dinosaurs; neither were plesiosaurs, ichthyosaurs or Dimetrodon. Birds, though, are the flying dinosaurs of today.
fact: All dinosaurs hatched from eggs; even the biggest eggs were only about the size of a football, because a much bigger egg would need a shell too thick for the baby to break out, and Maiasaura, the good mother lizard, nested in colonies and seems to have fed its young.
fact: Ankylosaurus was covered in bony plates, even on its eyelids, and swung a heavy bony tail club that could break a leg bone.
fact: Spinosaurus lived in North Africa about 95 million years ago with a back sail and a crocodile-like snout for fish; scientists still argue how well it swam, and it never met T. rex.
fact: Compsognathus was about 1 metre long including its tail, and the feathered Microraptor was about the size of a crow, with feathers on its arms and legs.
fact: The first humans of our kind appeared only about 300,000 years ago, and Stegosaurus lived longer before T. rex than T. rex lived before us.
exercise: About how long was Tyrannosaurus rex? | About 12 metres | About 3 metres | About 50 metres | About 1 metre
exercise: What could T. rex's thick, banana-shaped teeth do? | Crush bone | Chew grass | Crack only small nuts | Suck up water
exercise: What is true about the arms of T. rex? | They were short but thick and strong, with two fingers each | They were long and thin, with five long clawed fingers on each hand | They were too weak to lift a cup | They were used for walking on all fours
exercise: What did Stegosaurus have on its tail? | Four spikes for defence | A bony club like a hammer | A sail of skin | A long feathery fan
exercise: What is the truth about Stegosaurus's «second brain»? | It is a myth; the space in its hips probably held something else | It really had a second brain at the base of its tail to work its back legs | It had a brain in each leg | It had no brain at all
exercise: Which dinosaur had three horns and a big bony frill? | Triceratops | Stegosaurus | Ankylosaurus | Diplodocus
exercise: What shows that Triceratops fought T. rex and sometimes survived? | Healed T. rex bite marks on Triceratops bones | Cave paintings made by early people showing their battles | Broken T. rex teeth in Triceratops nests | Their footprints walking side by side
exercise: Why did sauropods like Diplodocus have such long necks? | To reach lots of plants without moving their heavy bodies | To breathe the clean air high up above the dust of the ground | To fight other dinosaurs by whipping | To look out for the asteroid coming
exercise: How big was the real Velociraptor? | About the size of a turkey | About the size of a horse | About the size of a bus | About the size of a mouse
exercise: What did the real Velociraptor have on its body? | Feathers | Smooth green scales only | Thick fur like a bear | Bony plates like a tank
exercise: Which of these was a dinosaur? | Triceratops | A pterosaur | A plesiosaur | Dimetrodon
exercise: What were pterosaur wings made of? | Skin stretched along one very long finger | Feathers on arms like a bird's | Skin stretched between all five fingers like a bat | Thin bony plates like a fan
exercise: How big were the biggest dinosaur eggs? | About the size of a football | About the size of a car | About the size of a fridge | About the size of a bathtub
exercise: What does the name Maiasaura mean? | Good mother lizard | Terrible lizard | Thunder lizard | Swift thief
exercise: What did Ankylosaurus use its tail club for? | Defence, a swing could break a leg bone | Digging deep holes in the ground for its eggs | Knocking fruit off trees | Steering while it swam
exercise: What did Spinosaurus mainly eat? | Fish | Leaves | Insects | Other Spinosaurus eggs
exercise: About how long was Compsognathus, including its tail? | About 1 metre | About 10 metres | About 10 centimetres | About 30 metres
exercise: When did dinosaurs live? | From about 230 to 66 million years ago | From about 2,300 to 660 years ago | From about 23 to 6 million years ago | From about 230,000 years ago to today
test: In a film, huge scaly Velociraptors as tall as a man open doors. What is right? | Real Velociraptor was turkey-sized and feathered | That is exactly what Velociraptor was like, the film got it right | Velociraptor was even bigger, the size of a T. rex | Velociraptor was a flying reptile
test: «T. rex had tiny arms coz it was weak, lol» What is right? | Its arms were short but strong; its huge jaws did the hunting | Yes, T. rex was too weak to hunt anything and only ate dead animals | Yes, T. rex arms could not even lift a pencil | No, T. rex had long arms for climbing trees
test: «pterodactyls are flying dinosaurs innit» What is right? | Pterosaurs were flying reptiles, not dinosaurs; birds are the flying dinosaurs | Yes, every flying reptile was a dinosaur, and pterosaurs were the biggest ones | Yes, and they were the first birds | No, they were giant bats
test: «T. rex vs Spinosaurus, who won the fight» What is true? | They never met: different continents, millions of years apart | T. rex won every time because it had the stronger bite of the two | Spinosaurus won every time | They were best friends in the same swamp
test: What would happen to a dinosaur egg as big as a car? | The shell would be too thick for the baby to break out | It would hatch a baby twice the size of any other dinosaur ever seen | It would roll away and hatch on its own | Nothing, big eggs are best
test: «Stegosaurus and T. rex were mates, saw it in a cartoon» What is right? | Stegosaurus lived longer before T. rex than T. rex lived before us | They lived at the same time and in the same place, just like the cartoon | Stegosaurus came after T. rex | They both lived only 1,000 years ago
test: Why might a huge sauropod want to stand still while eating? | Moving such a heavy body costs a lot of energy | It was too lazy to walk anywhere once it had found food | It could only eat with its legs locked | Its neck fell off if it walked
test: «all dinosaurs were massive, end of» What is right? | Many were small, like the chicken-sized Compsognathus | Yes, the smallest dinosaur was about the size of an elephant | Yes, every dinosaur was longer than a bus | No, all dinosaurs were tiny
test: A plant-eater with armour plates even on its eyelids and a club on its tail. Which is it? | Ankylosaurus | Stegosaurus | Triceratops | Diplodocus
test: «dinosaurs had live babies like us» What is right? | All dinosaurs hatched from eggs; fossil nests are found worldwide | Yes, only the very biggest ones laid eggs, the rest had live babies | Yes, dinosaurs had no eggs at all | No, they grew from seeds
test: «shut up, the ocean dinos like plesiosaurs were the biggest dinosaurs» What is right? | Plesiosaurs were sea reptiles, not dinosaurs; sauropods were the biggest | Yes, plesiosaurs were the biggest dinosaurs, bigger than any on land | Yes, all sea reptiles were dinosaurs | No, the biggest dinosaur was Velociraptor
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dimensions and integument of Velociraptor? | It was approximately the size of a turkey and was covered in feathers | It was approximately the size of a horse and was covered in thick scales | It was approximately the size of a bus and covered in plates | It was approximately the size of a mouse and covered in fur
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of the pterosaurs. | The pterosaurs were flying reptiles distinct from the dinosaurs | The pterosaurs were the direct ancestors of all modern birds | The pterosaurs were feathered dinosaurs belonging to the theropod group | The pterosaurs were flying mammals related to the bats
exam: Identify the defensive structure situated at the terminus of the tail of Ankylosaurus. | A heavy bony club | Four elongated spikes | A tall sail of skin | A fan of long feathers
exam: Determine the approximate interval during which the dinosaurs inhabited the Earth. | From approximately two hundred and thirty to sixty-six million years before the present | From approximately two thousand three hundred to six hundred and sixty years before the present | From approximately twenty-three to six million years before the present | From approximately two hundred and thirty thousand years ago to the present
